WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.705
Sixteen panels, six models, and the new one in bold blue on

00:00:03.705 --> 00:00:04.791
every one of them.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.706
Alibaba's Qwen4-Max launched today.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.241
No weights, no report.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:12.079
A paragraph, a diagram, a price list, and this chart.

00:00:15.127 --> 00:00:15.964
Hi, I'm Thatch.

00:00:15.964 --> 00:00:19.868
Let's read the machine first, then check every bar, then work out the bill.

00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:26.638
The total is the same as the last flagship, two point four trillion.

00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:31.269
What changed is how much each token touches: seventy-one billion, down from ninety-five.

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:33.406
A quarter less compute per token.

00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:36.613
And the machine is new.

00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:40.640
Three linear-attention layers, then one sparse-attention layer, repeated up the stack.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.821
Inside each layer, four residual lanes, read and written through gates.

00:00:45.201 --> 00:00:49.218
And at layer two, a lookup table keyed on short runs of tokens.

00:00:49.218 --> 00:00:52.616
It is the same drawing Qwen published for Flash-Next in August.

00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:56.003
Here is the problem.

00:00:56.003 --> 00:01:00.272
Full attention compares every new token with every old one, and keeps them all.

00:01:00.272 --> 00:01:03.017
At a million tokens that is the whole cost.

00:01:03.437 --> 00:01:07.949
Three layers in four fold the history into a fixed-size state instead.

00:01:07.949 --> 00:01:12.460
Cheap, constant, and lossy: a summary cannot always find one exact clause.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.171
So the fourth layer goes back to the real tokens.

00:01:16.171 --> 00:01:20.779
A light indexer scores micro-blocks of four tokens, which makes the choosing itself cheaper.

00:01:21.199 --> 00:01:23.730
Then it attends only inside the best blocks.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:28.159
Past the budget, the only part still growing with context is that cheap scan.

00:01:30.225 --> 00:01:31.688
Follow one token up the stack.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:36.545
At layer two it picks up a vector from a three hundred

00:01:36.545 --> 00:01:38.095
and twenty-eight billion parameter table.

00:01:38.095 --> 00:01:41.815
A lookup, not a multiply, so it can live in host memory.

00:01:42.215 --> 00:01:45.090
Three cheap memory layers, one sparse attention layer.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.246
Then the experts, seventy-one billion of them active.

00:01:48.646 --> 00:01:52.108
Everything passes through four gated lanes instead of one stream.

00:01:53.742 --> 00:01:55.374
It trained cleanly at that scale.

00:01:55.374 --> 00:01:58.637
But the curve has no token count, and there is no word

00:01:58.637 --> 00:02:00.540
on data or the R L recipe.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:03.340
Now the chart.

00:02:08.892 --> 00:02:12.087
Sixteen panels, with the new model in bold in every one.

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:14.758
It has the highest bar in four.

00:02:14.758 --> 00:02:17.009
In the rest, usually Opus is ahead.

00:02:17.009 --> 00:02:18.938
It beats its own predecessor everywhere.

00:02:20.575 --> 00:02:22.323
Terminal-Bench is the odd row.

00:02:22.323 --> 00:02:25.819
Anthropic's own page puts Opus seven points higher than this.

00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:30.397
Every bar in this panel is Artificial Analysis's run, to the decimal.

00:02:30.397 --> 00:02:31.446
A fair source.

00:02:31.446 --> 00:02:33.895
The footnotes just do not say so.

00:02:35.725 --> 00:02:39.448
Checked against rival cards and public leaderboards, panel by panel.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:41.696
Seven match a named source.

00:02:41.696 --> 00:02:44.255
Two are Artificial Analysis's without a footnote.

00:02:44.255 --> 00:02:47.546
ProgramBench has two bars that differ from Moonshot's card.

00:02:47.546 --> 00:02:49.374
Six I could not check.

00:02:51.217 --> 00:02:55.788
On a neutral index it lands below all four rivals, including the open Kimi.

00:02:55.788 --> 00:02:59.706
Strong on document work, weak at terminal coding, and the least likely

00:02:59.706 --> 00:03:01.665
of them to invent an answer.

00:03:03.342 --> 00:03:04.085
Now the money.

00:03:09.533 --> 00:03:12.843
At the same effort it uses about half the tokens on Terminal-Bench,

00:03:12.843 --> 00:03:13.947
for a higher score.

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:15.474
These are output tokens.

00:03:15.474 --> 00:03:18.629
At four fifty a million, they cannot add up to the

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:19.776
claimed cost per task.

00:03:19.776 --> 00:03:21.784
Most of the bill is cached input.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.668
Qwen says a Work360 task cost four forty-three on the old model.

00:03:28.087 --> 00:03:31.113
Charge the same tokens at the new prices and it is

00:03:31.113 --> 00:03:32.213
already about two fifty.

00:03:32.213 --> 00:03:34.413
Most of the saving is the price list.

00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:38.070
Fewer tokens take it the rest of the way, to one sixty-seven.

00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:42.213
The case studies deserve the same care.

00:03:42.213 --> 00:03:47.191
This table claims ninety-five percent, and prints pending confirmation under its own result.

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:52.568
And the coolant run said to work entirely on its own has

00:03:52.568 --> 00:03:56.103
a person at hour nineteen, holding up a sample that changed colour

00:03:56.103 --> 00:03:57.576
and steering the next round.

00:03:59.183 --> 00:04:02.866
A hybrid design that scales cleanly, a mostly honest chart, and the

00:04:02.866 --> 00:04:04.400
cheapest list price on it.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.776
But it is verbose, so measure your own cost per task.

00:04:09.483 --> 00:04:13.321
Fixed memory plus sparse attention, a chart checked bar by bar, and

00:04:13.321 --> 00:04:15.241
a bill that depends on tokens.

00:04:15.241 --> 00:04:16.200
Thanks for watching!

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.543
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:18.543 --> 00:04:19.093
I'm Thatch.

00:04:19.093 --> 00:04:19.368
Bye!
